    { level: 19, q: 'Roughly how long does research suggest it can take for a new behaviour to become automatic?', a: 'Around 2-3 months on average (highly variable)', d: ['Around 2-3 months on average (highly variable)', 'Exactly 21 days', 'Exactly 30 days', 'Exactly 100 days'], why: 'A widely-cited study found automaticity took a median of 66 days, with a huge range depending on the behaviour and person \u2014 not the popular \u201c21 days\u201d myth.' },
    { level: 23, q: 'Removing friction (e.g. laying out gym clothes the night before) mainly helps habit formation by\u2026', a: 'Making the desired behaviour easier to start', d: ['Making the desired behaviour easier to start', 'Increasing willpower', 'Adding a reward', 'Increasing motivation'], why: 'BJ Fogg\u2019s behaviour model shows that reducing the ability barrier is often more effective than trying to boost motivation.' },
    { level: 27, q: 'According to B.J. Fogg\u2019s Behavior Model, behaviour occurs when three elements converge at the same moment. What are they?', a: 'Motivation, ability and a prompt', d: ['Motivation, ability and a prompt', 'Motivation, reward and punishment', 'Cue, craving and response', 'Goal, plan and action'], why: 'Fogg\u2019s model (B=MAP) states a prompt only leads to behaviour when motivation and ability are both sufficiently high.' },
    { level: 31, q: 'Breaking a large goal into very small, almost-too-easy first steps (e.g. \u201cone push-up\u201d) is a strategy for\u2026', a: 'Overcoming the ability barrier to build momentum', d: ['Overcoming the ability barrier to build momentum', 'Increasing scarcity', 'Anchoring', 'Social proof'], why: '\u201cTiny habits\u201d lower the ability threshold so much that starting requires almost no willpower, building consistency first.' },
    { level: 36, q: 'A habit that becomes automatic is largely driven by which part of the brain?', a: 'The basal ganglia', d: ['The basal ganglia', 'The prefrontal cortex', 'The amygdala', 'The cerebellum'], why: 'As behaviours become habitual, control shifts from the deliberate prefrontal cortex to the more automatic basal ganglia.' },
    { level: 41, q: 'Relapsing into an old habit after a change attempt is best understood, in modern habit research, as\u2026', a: 'A normal, expected part of the change process, not a failure', d: ['A normal, expected part of the change process, not a failure', 'Proof that change is impossible', 'A sign of low intelligence', 'Irrelevant to future success'], why: 'The Stages of Change model treats relapse as a common loop back to earlier stages, not a permanent failure.' },
    { level: 45, q: 'Unfinished tasks tend to stick in your memory and nag at your attention far more than completed ones \u2014 this is\u2026', a: 'The Zeigarnik effect', d: ['The Zeigarnik effect', 'The overjustification effect', 'The mere-exposure effect', 'Cognitive dissonance'], why: 'Bluma Zeigarnik, a Soviet psychologist, noticed waiters remembered unpaid orders far better than paid ones \u2014 an open loop keeps demanding mental resources until it\u2019s resolved. It\u2019s why to-do lists reduce anxiety (writing a task down lets the brain "close the loop" without finishing it) and why a cliffhanger keeps you watching.' }
